    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's the thing—the Apollo samples are only from the crust. they didn't drill through the whole thing. if you look at the seismic data from the 90s, there are these weird \"echoes\" that suggest a large cavity. if the density is an average, it could be a thin shell of heavy material over a void. if i assume the shell is x thickness, how would the gravitational oscillation change? i need to see if the math supports a hollow interior despite the surface density readings.",
